      <title>Championship roundup: Swansea top after beating Norwich; Burnley put four past Watford</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/10/championship-roundup-swansea-norwich-birmingham-west-brom-middlesbrough-wolves</link>
      <description>&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Middlesbrough go second with win over Wolves&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Burnley off bottom; Birmingham beat West Brom&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;Vítor Matos praised his &lt;strong&gt;Swansea&lt;/strong&gt; side for overcoming a lack of training as they strengthened their position at the top of the Championship with a 2-1 comeback win over &lt;strong&gt;Norwich&lt;/strong&gt;. Second-half goals from Moussa Yeo and the Republic of Ireland striker Adam Idah wiped out Jacob Wright’s strike just before the half-hour mark.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It was the third time Swansea had come from behind this season to win, and, after &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/09/west-ham-qpr-championship-report-penalties"&gt;West Ham’s draw the previous night&lt;/a&gt;, it gave them top spot in the table. Idah, though, limped out of the action and could be a doubt for Tuesday’s visit to Millwall.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/10/championship-roundup-swansea-norwich-birmingham-west-brom-middlesbrough-wolves"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
